    "result_text": "Done. The full build passes with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nTelephone numbers are now stored in E.164 form. Three small, cohesive changes:\n\n**`NormalizeTelephone.java`** \u2014 rewrote the single `service` method to build E.164: strip non-digits; if the raw value starts with `+`, keep it as the country code; otherwise prepend `+61` after dropping one leading `0` from the national digits. Require 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, else reject with 400 (`MissingFieldsException`).\n\n**`EnsureUniqueTelephone.java`** \u2014 since stored telephones are now E.164, duplicate detection compares the request's E.164 value directly against each owner's stored E.164 value; removed the now-redundant `normalize` helper.\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 relaxed the entity's bean-validation constraints on `telephone` from a 10-digit-only rule (`@Digits` + `^[0-9]{10}$`) to E.164 (`^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$`), which was the source of the persist-time `ConstraintViolationException` (500) until fixed.\n\nVerified against the acceptance tests: `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, and `12` \u2192 400.\n\nStructural impact was kept low: each edited function is a single-method (or near-single-method) class with little surrounding complexity, and no method grew in complexity \u2014 `EnsureUniqueTelephone` actually shed a method. The entity change touches only field annotations, not any method body.",
